{"schemaVersion": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-10-11T09:00:00Z", "eventType": "stageEvent", "workItem": "JIRA-9001", "workflowId": "WF-FEATURE", "stageId": "requirements", "persona": "persona-product-owner", "status": "entered", "humanCheckpoint": "none", "hard": true, "note": "Starting requirements."}
{"schemaVersion": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-10-11T09:45:00Z", "eventType": "stageEvent", "workItem": "JIRA-9001", "workflowId": "WF-FEATURE", "stageId": "requirements", "persona": "persona-product-owner", "status": "complete", "humanCheckpoint": "none", "hard": true, "note": "ACs written: segment rules, retention cohorts, export limits.", "detail": "Acceptance Criteria:\n1. Given a customer with >=3 purchases in 90 days, when segmentation runs, then they are tagged 'frequent-buyer'.\n2. Given a customer with no login in 180 days, when segmentation runs, then they are tagged 'dormant' and excluded from active-customer counts.\n3. Given an admin exports a segment, when the export is requested, then the file is capped at 50,000 rows and a warning is shown above that.\n4. Given a customer matches more than one segment rule, when segments are computed, then all matching tags are applied (segments are non-exclusive).\n5. Given retention-cohort reporting is viewed, when a month is selected, then cohorts are grouped by signup month, not by current date.", "lawCitations": ["LAW-8.1"], "acceptanceCriteria": [{"id": "AC-1", "text": "Customers with >=3 purchases in 90 days are tagged 'frequent-buyer'."}, {"id": "AC-2", "text": "Customers with no login in 180 days are tagged 'dormant' and excluded from active-customer counts."}, {"id": "AC-3", "text": "Segment exports are capped at 50,000 rows with a warning shown above that."}, {"id": "AC-4", "text": "A customer matching multiple segment rules gets all matching tags (non-exclusive)."}, {"id": "AC-5", "text": "Retention-cohort reporting groups by signup month, not current date."}]}
{"schemaVersion": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-10-11T09:45:00Z", "eventType": "stageEvent", "workItem": "JIRA-9001", "workflowId": "WF-FEATURE", "stageId": "design", "persona": "persona-architect", "status": "entered", "humanCheckpoint": "none", "hard": true, "note": "Starting design."}
{"schemaVersion": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-10-11T09:50:00Z", "eventType": "deviation", "workItem": "JIRA-9001", "workflowId": "WF-FEATURE", "stageId": "design", "deviationStatus": "applied", "reason": "Single read-model table; no structural impact.", "approvedBy": "P. Polkampalli, 2026-10-11"}
{"schemaVersion": "1.0", "timestamp": "2026-10-11T11:00:00Z", "eventType": "stageEvent", "workItem": "JIRA-9001", "workflowId": "WF-FEATURE", "stageId": "design", "persona": "persona-architect", "status": "complete", "humanCheckpoint": "none", "hard": true, "note": "ADR-0019: segmentation engine as a read-model, no new write path.", "detail": "ADR-0019: Customer Segmentation Read-Model\n\nContext: Product wants segment tags (frequent-buyer, dormant, etc.) available in the CRM admin UI without changing how orders/logins are written today.\n\nDecision: Build segmentation as a nightly-refreshed read-model table (customer_segments) computed from existing orders/sessions tables. No new write path, no change to the transactional schema.\n\nAlternatives considered: (a) compute segments on-the-fly per request \u2014 rejected, too slow for the admin list view at current customer volume; (b) write segment tags directly onto the customers table on every order/login event \u2014 rejected, couples segmentation logic into the hot write path for no real benefit since segments only need to be eventually consistent.\n\nConsequences: segment tags can lag up to 24h behind real activity (acceptable per product); adds one new nightly batch job to monitor (ops picks this up in release-readiness).", "lawCitations": ["LAW-4.5"]}
